# 50: a spread template's key(), read through a reference to a SIBLING
# subtree four levels down, refuses in TypeScript and answers in Go.
#
# run: node ../../../ts/bin/aontu.js spread-key-through-deep-ref.aon
# run: aontu-go spread-key-through-deep-ref.aon
#
# expected: one answer, both ports.
#
# actual (Go):
#   {"a":{"b":{"c":{"d":{"e":{"x":{"n":"x"}}}},"f":{"x":{"n":"x"}}}}}
# actual (TypeScript):
#   [aontu/scalar_value]: Cannot unify values at path $.a.b.f.x.n.n
#    Cannot unify value: "n" with value: "x"
#
# THE PATH IS THE DIAGNOSIS. `$.a.b.f.x.n.n` carries `n` twice: the
# template is applied a SECOND time inside the field it already
# resolved. `n: key()` answers "x" at $.a.b.f.x.n, the template then
# meets that string as though it were a map, and the inner key()
# answers "n". At five levels the path reads $.a.b.f.x.n.e.n -- the
# referring path's own tail spliced in, which says the re-application
# is driven by the REFERENCE, not by the spread.
#
# It is a DEPTH THRESHOLD, not the shape: with `a: b: e:` or
# `a: b: c: e:` as the destination both ports agree. A threshold at
# four is a fixpoint-pass artefact, not a rule anyone wrote.
#
# Found by ADR-009's removal of `.$KEY`: the only test covering this
# shape used that spelling, which took the RefVal path and never
# reached the divergence.
a: b: c: d: e: $.a.b.f
a: b: f: &: {n: key()}
a: b: f: {x: {}}

# FIXED 2026-08-30: Go was right. A spread template now takes a stable
# identity across clones, so the bag loops' apply-once mark survives a
# reference's clone and the template is not applied a second time over
# the value the first application produced. Pinned by
# test/spec/gen-key.tsv key-spread-through-ref-2..5.
